        <p>
          In the high-stakes world of industrial chemical manufacturing and asset protection, the premature failure of structural steel due to atmospheric corrosion represents an enormous financial and structural liability. Conventional barrier coatings attempt to isolate metal surfaces from water, oxygen, and corrosive ions; however, microscopic pinholes, thermal cracking, and mechanical abrasion eventually compromise these barrier layers. Once moisture penetrates the film, an active, self-sustaining corrosion cell is established underneath the paint, leading to rapid delamination and loss of structural integrity.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <section class="section" id="toc" style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 24px; border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 40px;">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; background: none !important; color: var(--text-primary) !important; padding: 0 !important; font-size: 1.5rem !important; border: none !important;">In This Article</h2>
        <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin: 0; line-height: 1.8;">
