      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. How does zinc phosphate active corrosion protection differ from barrier protection?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Unlike simple physical barrier protection that only blocks moisture and oxygen from contacting the metal substrate, zinc phosphate provides active chemical passivation. When water penetrates the coating film, it triggers a controlled hydrolysis that releases phosphate ions. These ions migrate to the steel surface and react to form a highly insoluble crystalline passivation layer, shutting down the electrochemical corrosion process.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Why is zinc phosphate widely preferred over legacy zinc chromate primers?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Zinc chromate is highly toxic, carcinogenic, and strictly regulated under international standards like REACH and RoHS. Zinc phosphate offers a non-toxic, environmentally compliant alternative that provides robust anti-corrosive performance on steel substrates without the extreme occupational and ecological hazards associated with hexavalent chromium.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. What is the role of pH in the corrosion protection mechanism of zinc phosphate?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              The hydrolysis of zinc phosphate and the subsequent passivation reaction are highly pH-dependent. Under slightly acidic conditions, which typically occur at anodic corrosion sites on steel, the solubility of zinc phosphate increases, speeding up the release of active phosphate and zinc ions. These ions then precipitate at the metal interface to establish the protective crystalline barrier.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. Can zinc phosphate primers be used directly on non-ferrous metals like aluminum?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              While zinc phosphate primers are primarily optimized for steel, they can be formulated for non-ferrous surfaces like aluminum. However, to ensure adequate adhesion and optimal protection, non-ferrous metals usually require specialized surface preparation or conversion coatings before primer application to prevent adhesion failures.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. How does Pigment Volume Concentration (PVC) affect zinc phosphate primer efficacy?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              The Pigment Volume Concentration (PVC) must be carefully optimized relative to the Critical Pigment Volume Concentration (CPVC). If the PVC is too low, the release rate of active phosphate ions will be insufficient to establish passivation. If the PVC exceeds the CPVC, the coating becomes porous, allowing rapid water and chloride ingress that overwhelms the active anti-corrosive chemistry.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. What are common synergistic co-inhibitors used with zinc phosphate?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Formulators often combine zinc phosphate with synergistic additives like zinc oxide, organic silanes, and calcium ion-exchanged silica. These co-inhibitors help stabilize the film, improve wet adhesion, and accelerate the formation of the insoluble passivation layer, particularly in harsh environments classified under ISO 12944.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
